        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Yes, you can pour concrete when rain is likely, but not into standing water or a downpour, and the fresh surface has to be covered until it sets: light rain is usually harmless a few hours after finishing, while heavy rain needs closer to a full day. Cold is the more serious limit, because concrete gains strength slowly below 50°F, needs cold-weather protection once air temperatures drop toward 40°F, and should never be placed on frozen ground. Rain mostly threatens the top eighth of an inch, the part you walk on and see. Freezing threatens the whole slab. Plan around both and a wet forecast does not have to cancel the pour.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Can You Pour Concrete When It's Raining?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Concrete does not need dry weather to harden. It hardens through hydration, a chemical reaction between cement and water, and it will cure perfectly well under a gray sky. What rain damages is the surface while it is still plastic. Extra water landing on a freshly floated slab thins the cement paste at the top, and when that diluted paste hardens it is weaker, dustier and more prone to scaling than the concrete underneath it.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the honest answer has three parts. Before the pour, light rain is fine as long as the forms are not holding standing water and the subgrade is firm rather than muddy. During the pour, a drizzle can be managed if you have plastic sheeting ready, but a steady rain usually means stopping work on the surface. After finishing, the slab needs a few hours of protection from light rain and closer to a day of protection from a heavy one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Professional crews pour in marginal weather all the time. The difference between a crew that gets away with it and a homeowner who ends up with a pitted patio is preparation: poly sheeting cut to size and weighted down, a plan for where runoff from higher ground will go, and the discipline not to trowel rainwater into the surface. If you cannot have all three in place, the forecast is telling you to reschedule.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Water inside the mix is the enemy; water on a set slab is a friend.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Rain that gets worked into fresh concrete weakens the surface. Rain that falls after the concrete has set actually helps it cure, the same way a contractor would mist a slab or cover it with wet burlap.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Temperature changes the math more than rain does. A slab poured at 70°F reaches initial set in a few hours, so its window of vulnerability is short. The same slab poured at 45°F can stay soft for most of a day, which means it is exposed to rain for longer and is also at risk of freezing overnight. That is why the rest of this guide treats rain and cold together: both are about protecting concrete during the hours before it can protect itself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-long" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Long Does Concrete Need Before It Can Get Rained On?
          </h2>

          <p>
            There is no single number, because set time depends on the mix, the temperature and any admixtures. As a working rule, a normal mix poured in mild weather is usually safe from light rain about four to eight hours after finishing, once the surface is hard enough that you cannot press a thumbprint into it or scratch it easily with a fingernail. Heavy rain, which can pit and erode a surface that is only just firm, calls for 12 to 24 hours of cover.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Conditions after finishing</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Light rain usually OK after</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Heavy rain usually OK after</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Warm, 70°F and up</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 2 to 4 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 8 to 12 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Mild, 50 to 70°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 4 to 8 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 12 to 24 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cool, 40 to 50°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 8 to 12 hours or more</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 hours or more</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Below 40°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cold-weather protection needed regardless</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cover and insulate; check the forecast for freezing</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Treat the table as a starting point rather than a guarantee. A high-early-strength mix or an accelerator shortens the window, and a retarder, a very wet mix or deep shade lengthens it. The reliable test is the surface itself. If water beads and runs off without leaving marks, and a fingernail scratch barely registers, light rain will not hurt it. If the surface still feels slightly soft or looks wet and glossy, it needs cover.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bagged concrete behaves the same way as ready-mix. A fence post set with a fast-setting mix can take rain within an hour or so, because the product is formulated to set quickly and the exposed area is tiny. A small slab or step poured from standard bags follows the same timeline as a truck-delivered pour. Most mixes in our <a href="/best-concrete-mix/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete mix guide</a> list a set time on the bag, and that figure assumes roughly 70°F.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once the slab is past this early stage, rain stops being a risk and becomes part of curing. Concrete keeps gaining strength for weeks, reaching most of its design strength around 28 days, and keeping it damp during the first week helps. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-does-concrete-take-to-cure/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long concrete takes to cure</a> covers when you can walk, drive and build on it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mid-pour" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Should You Do If It Starts Raining Mid-Pour?
          </h2>

          <p>
            First, keep placing concrete if the rain is light, because a half-poured slab creates a cold joint that is harder to fix than a few raindrops. Get the concrete into the forms, screed it and bull float it, then cover the finished sections with plastic sheeting as you go. Keep the plastic off the surface with a few boards or stakes if you can, since sheeting laid directly on soft concrete leaves wrinkles and dark blotches.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not finish rainwater into the slab. If water collects on the surface, wait for the rain to ease and gently drag it off the edge with a hose or a length of lumber drawn across the forms, rather than troweling it in. Troweling pulls that water into the top layer and creates exactly the weak, dusty surface you are trying to avoid. A slightly rougher broom finish applied a little later is far better than a smooth one built on diluted paste.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">AVOID THIS</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Never sprinkle dry cement on a wet surface.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">It looks like it soaks up the water, but it leaves a thin, cement-rich skin that does not bond well and tends to craze, dust and peel. Remove the water instead.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Watch the edges and low spots. Runoff from a lawn, a roof or a sloped driveway can pour into the forms and wash out paste along one side, which leaves exposed aggregate and a weak edge. Before the truck arrives, check where water goes when it rains and dig a small diversion trench or set a row of sandbags if needed. Downspouts that dump near the pour should be redirected for the day.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the rain turns heavy before the slab is finished, stop finishing and cover it. Finishing can resume once the rain passes, provided the surface is still workable. If it has stiffened too much, accept a rougher texture. The structural slab underneath will be fine, and a light grind or a thin resurfacing coat later is cheaper than tearing out a pour because someone fought the weather with a steel trowel.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="damage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Does Rain Damage Concrete Permanently, or Just the Surface?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most rain damage is cosmetic and confined to the top eighth to quarter inch. Typical signs are pitting where drops struck soft paste, a sandy texture where paste washed away, dusting when you sweep the surface, and patches where the finish looks different from the rest of the slab. These do not usually change how much load the slab can carry, but they do make the surface more vulnerable to freeze-thaw scaling and deicing salt later.
          </p>

          <p>
            Structural damage is rarer and happens when a lot of water gets into the mix itself. That can occur if a downpour fills the forms during placement, if rainwater pools and is worked deep into the concrete, or if a crew adds water on site to keep a soggy mix moving. More water raises the water-to-cement ratio, and a higher ratio means lower strength and more shrinkage cracking throughout the slab, not just on top.
          </p>

          <p>
            To check the surface, wait about a week and scratch it firmly with a key or screwdriver. Sound concrete resists and leaves only a faint line. Weak surface paste scratches easily and leaves loose powder. Sweep a test area hard with a stiff broom; if it keeps producing fine dust, the top layer is weak. For a driveway, garage floor or structural element where you suspect the whole mix was compromised, an engineer or testing lab can take core samples.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fixing surface damage is usually straightforward. Light pitting can be left alone or treated with a penetrating sealer to reduce water uptake. A dusting or rough surface can be ground lightly and then covered with a polymer-modified resurfacer, which bonds to sound concrete and gives you a fresh wearing surface. Our <a href="/best-concrete-resurfacer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete resurfacer picks</a> cover the products that bond best over thin repairs.
          </p>

          <p>
            The one thing not to do is ignore a weak surface on an exterior slab in a cold climate. Diluted paste is exactly what scales first when water soaks in, freezes and expands. If rain roughed up a new driveway or walk, consider sealing it after it has cured, and go easy on deicers during its first winter. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-prevent-concrete-spalling-in-winter/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">preventing concrete spalling in winter</a> explains the steps.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-cold" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Cold Is Too Cold to Pour Concrete?
          </h2>

          <p>
            There is no absolute cutoff, because contractors pour in freezing weather with heated enclosures and insulated blankets. For a homeowner or a typical small crew, though, the practical lines are clear. Above 50°F, with no frost in the forecast, is ideal. Between about 40°F and 50°F, concrete can be poured but sets slowly and needs protection. Below about 40°F, or with freezing temperatures expected in the first couple of days, you are in cold-weather concreting territory and need a deliberate plan.
          </p>

          <p>
            The American Concrete Institute's cold-weather guidance, ACI 306, essentially treats conditions as cold weather when air temperature has fallen, or is expected to fall, below about 40°F during the protection period. The core requirement is that fresh concrete must not freeze before it reaches roughly 500 psi of compressive strength. Under normal conditions that usually takes about two days, but at low temperatures it can take considerably longer.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Air temperature</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What happens</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What to do</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">70°F and above</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Normal set and strength gain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Standard pour; watch for fast drying on hot, windy days</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">50 to 70°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Comfortable working window</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ideal for most DIY and residential pours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">40 to 50°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Set and early strength gain slow noticeably</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pour in the morning, use blankets overnight, consider an accelerator</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Below 40°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cold-weather conditions; freezing risk</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Insulate, consider warm-water or accelerated mixes, protect for several days</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ground frozen</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Subgrade will settle as it thaws</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Do not pour; thaw the ground first or wait</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The reason cold matters so much is that hydration slows sharply as temperature drops and crawls near freezing. A slab that would have been walkable the next morning at 70°F may still be soft at 40°F. If it freezes while still saturated, ice crystals form in the paste and expand, and that damage is permanent. Concrete that freezes early can lose a substantial share of its potential strength and durability, which is why cold is the more serious weather risk.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch the overnight low rather than the afternoon high. A 55°F afternoon followed by a 28°F night is a dangerous combination for a fresh slab, because the concrete will still be weak when the temperature drops. If the forecast shows freezing temperatures within 48 hours of the pour and you have no blankets or heat, wait for a warmer stretch.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cold-protection" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Do You Protect Concrete Poured in Cold Weather?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the ground. Never pour on frozen soil, frozen gravel or snow. Frozen subgrade settles as it thaws, which leaves the slab unsupported and cracked, and it pulls heat out of the concrete from below. Contractors thaw frozen ground with insulated blankets or ground heaters for a day or more before a pour. Clear any ice from forms and rebar too, since ice melts into extra water exactly where you do not want it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, warm the concrete itself. Ready-mix plants can batch with heated water and sometimes heated aggregate so the concrete arrives warm, and you can ask for this when you order in cold months. They can also add an accelerating admixture. Calcium chloride is the traditional accelerator and is typically limited to no more than about 2 percent by weight of cement; non-chloride accelerators are the usual choice where there is rebar or mesh, because chloride promotes steel corrosion.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then hold the heat in. Concrete produces its own heat as it hydrates, and insulated curing blankets trap it. Cover the slab as soon as finishing is done, overlap the edges and weigh them down, and leave them on until the concrete has gained enough strength, usually several days in cold weather. Corners and edges lose heat fastest, so give them extra coverage. Straw under plastic is an old-school substitute, but proper blankets work better.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not use unvented fuel-burning heaters under a tarp directly over a fresh slab. Combustion exhaust contains carbon dioxide, which can react with the soft surface and leave it chalky and weak, a problem known as carbonation dusting. Vented heaters, electric heat or insulated blankets avoid it. Also skip deicers on the new slab once it is in service; fresh concrete should not see salt during its first winter.
          </p>

          <p>
            For small DIY jobs, the simplest cold-weather strategy is timing. Pour mid-morning on a day when the high is comfortably above 50°F and the next two nights stay above freezing, use warm tap water in bagged mixes, and cover the work with blankets or several layers of plastic and old moving pads by late afternoon. If you cannot get two frost-free nights, wait. Spring will come, and the slab will last decades longer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="decide" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Should You Pour or Reschedule the Concrete Truck?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Look at three numbers in the forecast: the chance and intensity of rain during the pour and the next six to twelve hours, the overnight lows for the next two to three days, and wind. Light showers with highs in the 60s are workable with sheeting on hand. A line of thunderstorms arriving during finishing is not. Overnight lows near or below freezing need blankets or a new date. Strong wind dries the surface faster and makes plastic hard to keep in place.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ready-mix logistics matter too. Industry standards generally expect a truck to discharge within about 90 minutes of batching, so a delay caused by a downpour on site can push a load past its useful life. Many suppliers charge for loads cancelled late or held too long on site. Call the dispatcher the day before and the morning of the pour, ask about their weather policy, and do not be shy about moving the date. Rescheduling is cheaper than a ruined slab.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are hiring a contractor, ask how they handle rain and cold before you sign. A good answer includes covering materials they keep on the truck, a willingness to delay, a plan for cold-weather blankets or accelerators, and a commitment not to add water to make a stiff mix easier to place. A contractor who says weather never matters is telling you something about how carefully they work.
          </p>

          <p>
            For DIY projects, the bar should be higher than for pros, because you have fewer hands and less experience recovering from surprises. Choose a dry, mild day with a clear forecast for the next 24 hours, have plastic and weights staged before you mix the first bag, and pick a mix suited to the job. A fast-setting product shortens the window for posts and small repairs, while a standard or high-strength mix suits slabs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our honest view is that rain gets more blame than it deserves and cold gets less. A covered slab shrugs off a shower within hours, but a slab that freezes on its first night never fully recovers. If you only have the patience to watch one number, watch the overnight low.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Troweling rainwater into the surface</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water sitting on fresh concrete should be removed, not finished in. Troweling it into the top layer dilutes the paste and leaves a weak, dusty surface that scales easily in winter. Wait for the rain to ease, pull the water off the edge, then finish. A broom finish over sound paste beats a glassy one over diluted paste.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sprinkling dry cement to soak up water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dusting dry cement onto a wet slab seems like a quick fix, but it creates a thin, cement-rich skin that does not bond well to the concrete underneath. It tends to craze, peel and dust within a season or two. Remove the water instead. If the slab is too stiff, accept the texture you have.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pouring on frozen ground</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Frozen subgrade feels solid, but it settles as it thaws and pulls heat out of the slab from below. The result is cracking, uneven support and slow curing. Thaw the ground with blankets or heaters first, or wait for a warmer stretch. Once the frost is out and the base is firm and compacted, pour.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Watching the high instead of the low</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A mild afternoon tells you little about whether the slab will survive. Fresh concrete is most vulnerable during its first cold night, so check overnight lows for the next two to three days and plan blankets or a new date accordingly. Two frost-free nights is the minimum to aim for.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Laying plastic directly on soft concrete</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sheeting dropped straight onto a fresh surface leaves wrinkles, blotches and uneven color, especially on decorative finishes. Hold it off the surface with boards or stakes when possible, and lay it once the surface can take light contact without marking. Wrinkle marks are permanent once the concrete hardens, and no amount of scrubbing will even them out.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can it rain the day after you pour concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually yes. By the next day a normal mix poured in mild weather has set hard enough that rain will not damage the surface, and the moisture actually helps curing. The exception is cold weather, when the slab may still be soft, and the bigger concern is whether the night will drop below freezing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should you cover new concrete if it rains?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, until the surface has set enough to resist a fingernail scratch. Use plastic sheeting held off the surface if you can, weight the edges, and make sure runoff from surrounding ground cannot flow under it. Once the slab is hard, uncovering it during rain is fine and helps keep it moist.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it OK if fresh concrete gets wet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Once it has set, getting wet is good for it, because concrete cures best when kept moist for the first week. Before it sets, extra water on the surface dilutes the cement paste and weakens the top layer. The timing, not the water itself, is what decides whether rain helps or hurts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What temperature should concrete be poured at?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Aim for air temperatures between about 50°F and 70°F, with no freezing in the forecast for two or three days. Concrete can be placed in colder conditions with blankets, warm mix water and accelerators, but those steps add cost and risk that most homeowner projects do not need to take on.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can concrete freeze after 24 hours?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Fresh concrete needs to reach roughly 500 psi before it can tolerate freezing, which typically takes about two days in normal conditions and longer in cold weather. A slab that freezes within the first day or so can suffer permanent damage, so keep it insulated until it has had time to gain strength.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does rain make concrete crack?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rain itself rarely causes structural cracks. Excess water in the mix can increase shrinkage cracking, and a weakened surface can craze into fine hairline cracks. Most cracks in new slabs come from shrinkage, poor joint spacing or a soft subgrade, so check those causes before blaming the weather. Weather is usually a minor factor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you pour concrete footings in the rain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Light rain is usually manageable for footings, since the surface finish does not matter, but trenches must not contain standing water or mud. Pump or bail out water first, and make sure the soil at the bottom is firm. If the trench walls are collapsing into the footing, wait for drier conditions.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

