        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Rain on its own rarely ruins a pour. A slab that is covered until it sets and never has water troweled into its surface will usually look and perform the same as one poured on a sunny day. The real damage happens when nobody has plastic ready, runoff floods the forms, or a crew tries to finish through a downpour.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Cold deserves more respect. Pour above 50°F when you can, protect anything poured near 40°F with blankets for several days, and never pour on frozen ground. If the forecast will not cooperate, move the date. A week's delay costs nothing compared with a slab that scales, dusts or cracks for the next thirty years.
          </p>
